  "query_preview": "..\nUpper riding will continue through tomorrow, bringing warm and dry\nconditions to the region. As the ridge begins to flatten the \npressure gradient will increase causing upper level winds to\nstrengthen. Assuming efficient mixing given warm temperatures and\nclear skies, breezy to gusty winds are likely at the surface. For\na brief period during the afternoon fire weather concerns will be\nincreased, notably in Petroleum and Philips counties. Confidence \nin a Red Flag Warning is low, especially given the short period of\nboth low RH and gusty winds. \n\nWith that said, with the maintenance of a chinook arch and lee \ntrough, the high temperature may be slightly lower than forecast \neven with the effects of prefrontal compressional warming. This \nwould raise min RH values and lessen fire weather concerns. \n\nBy Sunday a more zonal flow pattern returns with a brief drop in \ntemperatures behind a cold front. Weak riding returns for the\nbeginning of the week and upper level flow turns increasingly \nsoutheast early next week with an upper low digging south off the \nwest coast. By midweek flow will split with the upper low cutting \noff from the Canadian northern near zonal flow. Next chance for \nprecipitation appears to be late next week as the closed low moves\nonshore and begins to open up. This will allow it to phase with a\nnorthern stream trough and pull Pacific moisture northward \nthrough the Great Basin.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \nRaised day 1 temperatures and winds, and lowered RH. \n\nGiven the quiet weather through the week, confidence is generally\nhigh through forecast period. Uncertainty remains with any precip\nat the end of next week.",
